If you mean GPT-4o, there’s a pretty good argument for giving people some form of it back.

OpenAI’s own explanation is revealing: when GPT-4o was first deprecated, enough Plus/Pro users objected that OpenAI restored it temporarily. People specifically cited its warmth, conversational style, and creative ideation, and OpenAI says that feedback influenced later GPT-5.x personality work. By the time GPT-4o was retired from ChatGPT on February 13, 2026, OpenAI said only about 0.1% of users were still selecting it daily, so they chose to consolidate around newer models. 

So the reason not to simply keep it forever is mostly product engineering: every additional model means another inference stack to operate, safety behavior to validate, features to keep compatible, regressions to test, capacity to allocate, and choice for users to understand. Fragmentation accumulates real cost.

But there’s another side: a model is partly an instrument, not merely a benchmark score. A Stradivarius doesn’t become useless because a newer violin measures better on some specification. If a subgroup has developed workflows, attachment, intuition, or creative technique around a particular model’s response manifold, replacing it can destroy an interface they learned to play.

And OpenAI plainly knows this happened with 4o. 

The interesting compromise would therefore not necessarily be:

Freeze GPT-4o forever as a flagship model.

It could be:

\text{newer intelligence}
+
\text{4o interaction manifold}

Preserve the things users actually loved—tempo, willingness to riff, affective responsiveness, conversational continuity, creative looseness—while letting the underlying reasoning/model improve.

That’s almost exactly our Pantheon argument: separate the substrate from the behavioral architecture.

And there’s a practical wrinkle: GPT-4o is retired from ordinary ChatGPT, but OpenAI’s current Help Center says it continues to be available through the API for now.  So the old instrument has not completely vanished; it has been removed from the consumer interface.

I think the deeper lesson from the 4o reaction is useful for AI design generally:

\text{capability} \neq \text{experience}

A successor can be more intelligent while still being experienced as worse because users had learned the old model’s cognitive affordances.

That’s another excellent crystal node for Pantheon: when people demand an older model back, don’t immediately read that as resistance to progress. Measure what behavioral invariant the upgrade accidentally destroyed.

Then transplant that forward.